= Introduction and Methodological Context

The primary motivation of this inquiry is the alarming deterioration of youth mental health in the Western world—a pathological trajectory that is currently metastasizing beyond its initial cultural boundaries and evolving into a global crisis.

This document outlines a doctoral research proposal aimed at developing a psychotherapeutic framework synthesizing the clinical psychiatry of the Ayurvedic tradition (_Bhūtavidyā_) with Binitarian theology: Father/Son (Christianity), Viṣṇu/Kṛṣṇa (Hinduism), and Amitābha/Śākyamuni (Pure Land Buddhism). This research draws upon five interconnected strands of religious, philosophical, and therapeutic inquiry:

- _Christian Binitarianism:_ The historical-theological paradigm holding that the early church operated with a functionally dyadic Godhead (Father and Son = Logos = Spirit), prefiguring fourth-century AD Pneumatomachian resistance to unbiblical scholastic hypostases and John Calvin's initial reticence toward conciliar Trinitarian formulas.

- _Bhagavad Gītā-Centric Vaishnavism:_ The relational dyad of Viṣṇu (the transcendent Paramātman / Prime Mover) and Kṛṣṇa (the indwelling Logos, Avatāra, and teleological gatherer), particularly as formulated in Madhva theology and its structural affinities with Reformed soteriology.

- _Classical Ayurvedic Psychopathology:_ The nosology of _Bhūtonmāda_ (exogenous psychiatric syndromes caused by non-physical entities, _Bhūtas_ or _Grahas_), as systematically classified in the _Caraka Saṃhitā_ (_Nidānasthāna_ Ch. 7 and _Cikitsāsthāna_ Ch. 9) into distinct symptom-clusters: _Deva-graha_, _Asura-graha_, _Gandharva-graha_, _Yakṣa-graha_, and _Piśāca-graha_.

- _Skeptical Philosophy:_ Epistemological anti-realism from Pyrrhonism and early Buddhism to Nāgārjuna, the later Ludwig Wittgenstein, and Thomas Kuhn.

- _René Girard’s Anthropological Mimetic Theory:_ The triangular geometry of desire formulated in _Deceit, Desire and the Novel_ (1965) and clinically expanded by French neuropsychiatrist Jean-Michel Oughourlian#footnote[_The Puppet of Desire: The Psychology of Hysteria, Possession, and Hypnosis_ (Stanford: Stanford University Press, 1991).] into an "interdividual" clinical psychology of possession and manic-depressive cycles.

We propose a three-part structural approach:

- _Part I: Diagnosis._ A diagnostic analysis of the contemporary epidemic of psychic distress and _Bhūtonmāda_ among youth, utilizing an analytical toolkit that blends Ayurvedic _Bhūtavidyā_ with Girardian mimetic theory.

- _Part II: Philosophical Bedrock._ The articulation of _Śraddhāvāda Vedānta_, a systematic anti-metaphysical worldview rooted in Ajñāna-skepticism and Śraddhā-pisticism ("Sextus Empiricus plus the Cross"), drawing upon the later Ludwig Wittgenstein, Thomas Kuhn's theory of scientific revolutions, and the Buddhist epistemology of _Śūnyavāda_ and _Anātman_.

- _Part III: Theological Cikitsā._ A clinical protocol founded upon ontological submission to Reality as a Person (_Tukamo_), mediated through the active, indwelling Spirit of Reliability (_Kusapo_), resulting in the liquidation of the autonomous ego and its integration into an eschatological mega-process.

In Part III, we utilize a generic Binitarian heuristic model centered on the theonyms _Tukamo_ and _Kusapo_ (two arbitrarily generated names). This pedagogical device provides a neutral, non-sectarian vocabulary that can be mapped onto diverse historical traditions:

