= Part I — Diagnosis

In this section, we apply the historical-structural methodology developed in _McThelema: The Submerged Iceberg Below the Cornell 7 Case_#footnote[Nicolas Reimen, _McThelema: The Submerged Iceberg Below the Cornell 7 Case_ (2026, unpublished).] to establish the etiology of contemporary youth neurosis.

It is a commonplace in contemporary psychiatry to highlight the severe markers of acute psychic suffering among modern youth. Over the last three decades, the psychological baseline of young adults has experienced a catastrophic decline, manifested most acutely in a dramatic surge in youth suicides. According to data from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), the suicide rate among Americans aged 10 to 24 increased by 57.4% between 2007 and 2018 alone, solidifying its place as the second leading cause of death for this demographic. Concurrently, reliance on psychiatric pharmaceuticals has accelerated: between 2016 and 2022, the dispensing rate of antidepressants and anxiolytics for adolescents and young adults jumped by 66.3%, with prescribing rates for adolescent girls rising nearly 130% following the onset of the pandemic. This internal despair is mirrored by profound social withdrawal and relational atomization. Contrary to popular cultural narratives of rampant hedonism, Western youth are in the midst of a pronounced "sex recession." Sociological surveys consistently reveal declining sexual activity, noting that young adults today are significantly more likely to report having had zero sexual partners in the preceding year compared to prior cohorts. This retreat from intimacy extends to long-term civic institutions, as marriage rates have fallen to unprecedented historical lows: the U.S. national marriage rate dropped from 9.8 per 1,000 population in 1990 to 5.1 per 1,000 in 2020, with Census data showing that only 20.3% of men and 30.3% of women born in the early 1990s were married by age 25.#footnote[See Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), _State Suicide Rates Among Adolescents and Young Adults Aged 10–24: United States, 2000–2018_ (_National Vital Statistics Reports_, 2020); K. P. Chua et al., "Antidepressant Dispensing to US Adolescents and Young Adults: 2016–2022", _Pediatrics_ (2024); CDC National Center for Health Statistics, _National Marriage and Divorce Rate Trends_; and U.S. Census Bureau, _Current Population Survey_.]

In order to contextualize this macro-sociological collapse, we examine a specific, diagnostic index case: the Cornell 7 fraternity sexual encounter of October 2024 and its protracted judicial unfolding through late 2026. To contextualize this case, we will look at the background of female "clique" behavior in high schools and universities and the attendant phenomenon of the "Queen Bee" within those social hierarchies.

== I.1. The Cornell 7 Case and Collegiate Ritualized Group Sex

=== I.1.1 The "mean girls" context

Since the late 1980s and 1990s, the dynamics of covert relational aggression among adolescent females have been widely documented across developmental psychology, sociology, and education. This body of observation crystallized in Rosalind Wiseman's landmark text _Queen Bees and Wannabes: Helping Your Daughter Survive Cliques, Gossip, Boyfriends, and Other Realities of Girl World_ (2002),#footnote[Rosalind Wiseman, _Queen Bees and Wannabes: Helping Your Daughter Survive Cliques, Gossip, Boyfriends, and Other Realities of Girl World_ (New York: Crown Publishers, 2002).] which later formed the foundational architecture for the cultural touchstone film _Mean Girls_ (2004),#footnote[_Mean Girls_, directed by Mark Waters, screenplay by Tina Fey (Paramount Pictures, 2004).] alongside complementary empirical investigations such as Rachel Simmons’s _Odd Girl Out: The Hidden Culture of Aggression in Girls_ (2002).#footnote[Rachel Simmons, _Odd Girl Out: The Hidden Culture of Aggression in Girls_ (New York: Harcourt, 2002).] These studies laid bare an uncompromising, highly stratified social pyramid. At the apex sits the "Queen Bee," who wields social capital through an asymmetric economy of intimidation, conditional inclusion, and weaponized gossip. Subordinated to her are the "Wannabes" and secondary enforcers, who continuously shore up their own precarious status by orchestrating silent boycotts, reputational purges, and ruthless character assassination against anyone threatening the hierarchy.

The psychological toll inflicted by this continuous relational warfare is profound and lasting. Unlike direct physical violence, which leaves visible scars and invites institutional redress, relational aggression operates through ambiguous, non-physical terror—systematic gaslighting, public humiliation, and social isolation. For teenage girls navigating this panopticon, membership in the clique is an existential matter, transforming daily peer interaction into an exhausting exercise in self-monitoring and appeasement. This chronic, hyper-vigilant anxiety was poignantly captured in the genesis of Jeffrey Eugenides’s acclaimed 1993 novel _The Virgin Suicides_:#footnote[Jeffrey Eugenides, _The Virgin Suicides_ (New York: Farrar, Straus and Giroux, 1993).] the author revealed that the initial seed for the entire book was planted in the late 1980s by his nephew’s teenage babysitter, who remarked with eerie nonchalance that she and all her sisters had already attempted suicide simply because they were "under a lot of pressure."

When refracted through this vicious intra-female status competition, the resulting psycho-cultural mechanics strip the vast majority of young women of any intrinsic sense of self-worth. Those who are unable or unwilling to maintain the punishing aesthetic and social standards of the Queen Bee are discarded into a state of acute ontological bankruptcy. Borrowing Nietzschean categories, this mechanism drills into the psyche of countless adolescent girls the devastating conviction that they are "unf\*\*\*\*able _Untermenschen_" (or _Unterfrauen_)—worthless genetic and social castoffs forever excluded from love and desire. The predictable clinical consequence is a pervasive, chronic depressive mindset marked by dysmorphia, numbness, and acute suicidal ideation, creating an internal vacuum that primes these young women to submit to radical, self-destructive rituals once they transition into the collegiate arena.

=== I.1.2 The Cornell 7 facts

In American colleges, the "mean girls" dynamic continues to operate, often at an even higher level of intensity, through so-called "Greek Letter Sororities."#footnote[In the United States higher education system, "Greek-letter organizations" (fraternities for men and sororities for women) are exclusive, single-sex social societies that maintain private residential properties, esoteric initiation rites, and powerful alumni patronage networks across corporate and political life.] Alongside this intra-female sorting mechanism, the existence of equivalent male fraternities introduces a powerful structural stressor: the clandestine practice of organized, multi-partner fraternity semi-public group s\*\* encounters. The events that unfolded on the night of October 19, 2024, at the Chi Phi fraternity house at Cornell University (107 Edgemoor Lane in Ithaca, New York) serve as an empirical illustration of this phenomenon. According to official police records and sworn depositions, a female undergraduate (designated as Jane Doe in official documentation) arrived at the chapter house late in the evening and engaged in a multi-hour sequence of sexual acts with several high-status fraternity brothers while significantly intoxicated by alcohol and ketamine.

Following the initial reporting of the incident to Cornell University Police in November 2024, the case entered a protracted period of administrative and legal meandering. While an internal university Title IX inquiry was launched, the Tompkins County District Attorney's Office originally declined to pursue criminal indictments.#footnote[A lack of criminal indictment is a common occurrence in such cases because proving the crime of r\*\*\* "beyond a reasonable doubt" in the murky circumstances of collegiate partying presents extreme evidentiary hurdles.] In a subsequent public statement issued in late September 2026, the District Attorney defended the original decision by citing the plaintiff's initial sworn statements, which described her participation in substance use and sexual conduct as conscious and non-coerced, highlighting the severe ambiguities surrounding statutory definitions of consent in group encounters characterized by acute intoxication and porous room boundaries.

Despite the absence of initial criminal proceedings, the encounter triggered catastrophic relational consequences for the female student. In the immediate aftermath, details of the night circulated across campus digital networks, subjecting her to intense humiliation and the devastating label of being a "s\*\*\*." While the student had dimly recognized beforehand the reputational peril of entering an all-male fraternity setting under such circumstances, the lived reality of peer ostracism proved unbearable. The acute anguish of being publicly branded a repulsive outcast and reduced to an object of contempt by both fraternity men and female peers caused severe psychological decompensation, illustrating how the informal moral policing of the university community inflicts far swifter and harsher punishment than formal legal institutions.

The case remained dormant until September 2026, when Jane Doe filed a comprehensive federal civil lawsuit against the fraternity, local chapter leaders, and individual participants. The complaint detailed real-time digital coordination via group messaging applications, alleging that fraternity members systematically orchestrated the encounter and broadcasted graphic commentary. The filing ignited nationwide media scrutiny, student walkouts, and intense political backlash against the university administration, ultimately compelling the appointment of a special prosecutor to reopen the criminal investigation. This dramatic escalation transformed what had been treated as an isolated campus incident into a prominent national debate over institutional liability, consent ambiguity, and the entrenched subculture of collegiate Greek life. We believe that this backlash entirely misses the actual psycho-cultural mechanisms at play in such events and semi-consciously attempts to cover them up under criminal labelling. In other words, American culture is ashamed (and for good reason) of what its youth culture has become, using the criminal justice system to whitewash such revealing incidents as supposedly isolated "crimes."

=== I.1.3 Female "slut-shaming" Sorority culture

The mechanisms of campus s\*\*\*-shaming within elite collegiate ecosystems have been extensively analyzed in contemporary sociology, most notably in the landmark empirical research of Elizabeth Armstrong, Laura Hamilton, and their collaborators.#footnote[Elizabeth A. Armstrong, Laura T. Hamilton, Elizabeth M. Armstrong, and J. Lotus Seeley, "'Good Girls': Gender, Social Class, and Slut Discourse on Campus," _Social Psychology Quarterly_ 77, no. 2 (2014): 100–122.] Their work demonstrates that derogatory sexual discourse on campus is rarely driven by genuine moral indignation or traditional religious piety. Rather, it operates as a calculated weapon in status contests, where affluent women employ "classy" sexual standards to assert class advantage and social distinction over peers, while lower-status women weaponize resentment against those who violate normative boundaries.

Within the formal structure of elite sororities, this disciplinary control is institutionalized through internal judicial committees known as "Standards Boards." Operating much like modern corporate tribunals, these boards summon accused members behind closed doors under the pretext of "risk management," "preserving chapter standing," and "protecting the brand." Internal rivals frequently exploit rumors of sexual impropriety to initiate anonymous complaints, transforming disciplinary hearings into covert purges. By officially expelling a compromised member, the remaining sorority cohort performatively re-establishes its own purity and marriageability (although the significance of this second aspect is rapidly decreasing), sacrificing their peer to insulate the chapter's institutional standing, or so they say.

In the digital sphere, this reputational warfare is amplified and executed on anonymous forums such as GreekRank#footnote[GreekRank is an anonymous online forum where university students rate and rank collegiate fraternities and sororities, frequently serving as a digital venue for campus gossip, status competition, and peer surveillance.] and collegiate confession subreddits. These platforms serve as digital arenas where rival factions monitor and demolish peer status. When rumors emerge that an elite, attractive woman has participated in a fraternity group s\*\* encounter, users instantly strip her of her social standing, deploying degrading, infrastructural labels such as "the frat mattress" or mocking her for "taking the whole house." The digital record captures the total liquidation of the individual's social capital, leaving her completely isolated within the collegiate environment; as one expelled sorority member lamented on an anonymous forum, "_People who were my 'fam for life' barely even acknowledge me now when they see me out in public._"

Crucially, this online and offline destruction is often spearheaded by the aforementioned status-deprived _Unterfrauen_—women who feel excluded from the upper tiers of aesthetic and social prestige. Lacking the effortless social dominance of the campus "Queen Bees," these peers harbor deep-seated resentment. When a prominent, attractive peer falters by crossing the line into transgressive sexual behavior, it creates an opportune moment for collective vengeance. Under the virtuous alibi of enforcing community reputation, these peers orchestrate total social annihilation, gleefully dragging down an otherwise untouchable rival while congratulating themselves on their superior decorum and restraint.

This dynamic reveals the profound sociological paradox at the heart of campus sexual culture. For any affiliated sorority girl, participating in a fraternity party group s\*\* encounter is universally recognized as guaranteed reputational suicide—a fatal misstep that inevitably triggers expulsion from the sorority, peer condemnation, and social death, often ending with the student being forced to transfer to a less prestigious university. Yet, despite widespread awareness of these consequences, fraternities experience no shortage of female candidates willing to cross that threshold. This recurring phenomenon defies conventional models of rational self-interest and utilitarian choice, presenting a glaring contradiction that urgently requires an anthropological and psychological explanation.

=== I.1.4 The Historical Emergence of Frat Rituals

A genealogical analysis reveals that the collegiate group s\*\* encounter did not emerge spontaneously during the countercultural movements of the late 1960s; rather, it was already an established, codified institution in elite American universities by the late 1950s. As documented in the autobiographical writings of Chris Miller (a 1963 Dartmouth graduate whose recollections later inspired the 1978 film _National Lampoon's Animal House_), events such as the "Seven-Man Gang-B\*\*\*" were treated as revered chapter traditions, defined by explicit queuing protocols, numeric quotas, and strict secrecy under the administrative umbrella of _in loco parentis_.#footnote[A Latin legal doctrine meaning "in the place of a parent," historically granting colleges and universities discretionary authority over student conduct, which effectively functioned to insulate internal fraternity traditions from municipal law enforcement.] Prior to World War II, elite US senior societies such as Yale’s Skull and Bones maintained homosocial bonding rituals like "Connubial Bliss," which merely required initiates to verbally disclose their sexual histories to peers without physical exhibition. The mid-century emergence of performative, multi-partner sexual encounters marked a radical structural departure.

The historical transmission vector for this practice traces directly back to the geopolitical crucible of the Second World War. The Office of Strategic Services (OSS)—the wartime predecessor to the CIA—recruited heavily from Ivy League#footnote[The "Ivy League" refers to an elite athletic conference comprising eight historically prestigious and socially exclusive private research universities in the Northeastern United States: Brown, Columbia, Cornell, Dartmouth, Harvard, the University of Pennsylvania, Princeton, and Yale. In American sociological history, these institutions and their exclusive undergraduate fraternities and senior societies have served as primary incubators for the nation's political, diplomatic, and financial establishments.] fraternities. Stationed in wartime London, these young American operatives were deeply immersed in British aristocratic intelligence circles, where they absorbed the transgressive mores of figures associated with the Bloomsbury Group, the hedonistic Happy Valley Set in colonial Kenya, and the practical "sex magick" traditions pioneered by occultist Aleister Crowley and the Ordo Templi Orientis (O.T.O.). In these elite British networks, the deliberate breaking of bourgeois sexual taboos was utilized to forge intense homosocial solidarity and an untouchable sense of caste immunity. When these young American operatives returned to elite campuses after the war, they brought this codified, transgressive architecture with them, masking its aristocratic and esoteric roots behind the brutalist, industrial slang of the American working class ("running a train," designated "conductors").

This lineage explains the extraordinary status economy that continues to govern contemporary fraternity houses. Far from being viewed by their peers as moral deviants, male participants who coordinate and execute these encounters are rewarded with immense charismatic prestige within their organizations. Public digital records from platforms such as Reddit and GreekRank consistently reveal that chapter leaders who orchestrate multi-partner events are lionized in mythic vocabulary, with members recounting: "_There was a guy in our chapter, absolute legend, who we basically considered god-tier after homecoming weekend... Dude is literally in our chapter's hall of fame. He didn't buy a beer in this town for the rest of his senior year._" The public exhibitionism and the presence of an appreciative male audience serve to consecrate the leader's alpha-male standing, reinforcing the participants' status as untouchable "legends" within their peer ecosystem.

While this historical trajectory demonstrates the aristocratic origins and high social capital associated with these rituals—thereby offering an initial rationale for why female students might be drawn toward high-status campus male figures—it remains a fundamentally incomplete explanation. Social prestige alone cannot account for why a sorority girl would choose to participate in an act universally known to trigger devastating reputational annihilation, sorority expulsion, and campus exile. The sheer disproportion between the fleeting proximity to high-status males and the permanent social death that follows demonstrates that rational-choice or purely sociological frameworks fail to grasp the core problem. The persistent attraction to the fraternity ritual, capable of overriding the most severe social backlash, points beyond secular incentives to a deeper, unconscious intrapsychic and metaphysical imperative.

== I.2. Girardian _Bhūtonmāda_: Triangular and Metaphysical Desire

=== I.2.1 The structural metaphysics of Girardian Bhūtonmāda

The foundation of René Girard's theoretical framework, as originally formulated in his analysis of the European novel,#footnote[René Girard, _Mensonge romantique et vérité romanesque_ (Paris: Grasset, 1961); translated into English by Yvonne Freccero as _Deceit, Desire, and the Novel: Self and Other in Literary Structure_ (Baltimore: Johns Hopkins Press, 1965). We limit our use of Girardian concepts to those presented in this work, as we consider the subsequent notion of the _bouc émissaire_ (scapegoat) to be a dilution that obscures the original psychodynamic architecture.] begins with a deceptively simple geometric observation regarding human longing. Traditionally, romantic and philosophical thought has conceptualized desire as a linear dynamic consisting of two points: a desiring subject and a desired object. In this prevailing "_romantique_#footnote[We deliberately use the French word here as its English equivalent has semantic associations that would be misleading in this context. René Girard is not talking about 'romantic attraction' but about the illusion fostered by the _romantique_ mindset concerning the supposed spontaneity of human desires.]" illusion, as Girard puts it, the subject believes their desire is entirely spontaneous and autonomous, originating from within their own authentic self and directed toward an object whose inherent qualities naturally draw them in. Girard shatters this dyadic model by introducing a third point, the "mediator" or "model", thus forming a triangle. According to this triangular configuration, the subject does not actually desire the object for its own sake; rather, the subject desires the object because it is already desired—or possessed—by the mediator. Consequently, all desire#footnote[In _DD&TN_, the phrase 'all desire' must be considered with prudence. Of course, Girard does not pretend that a physiological reflex, such as the impulse to use the restroom, is imitated from a mediator. However, he insists on repeating 'all' because he wants to warn the reader that the proportion of human desires motivated by unconscious imitation is always far greater than we ordinarily acknowledge or realize.] is inherently borrowed, constituting what is known as "mimetic desire."

The relationship between the subject and the mediator dictates the intensity and the destructive potential of this imitative process. When the mediator is socially, historically, or culturally distant from the subject—such as a legendary hero, an inaccessible celebrity, or a literary figure—the relationship remains stable and is classified as "external mediation" (or "the Red"#footnote[These Girardian color-based categories are drawn from the title of a famous early nineteenth-century novel by Stendhal, _The Red and the Black_.]). In this state, the subject openly acknowledges the model and strives to emulate them without any risk of direct competition. However, when the mediator occupies the same social sphere as the subject, the dynamic transforms into "internal mediation" (or "the Black"). Because both individuals are now capable of pursuing the exact same object, the mediator simultaneously acts as a model to be imitated and a formidable rival to be defeated. This proximity breeds intense jealousy, secrecy, and resentment, as the subject is forced to hide their imitation while furiously competing for the identical prize.

As this rivalry within internal mediation intensifies, a profound psychological shift occurs, propelling the framework from a structural geometry of borrowed longing into the realm of "metaphysical desire." Initially, the subject's attention is entirely fixated on the physical object itself, while the mediator is merely viewed as an obstacle standing in the way of acquisition. However, as the competition escalates, the object gradually loses its intrinsic value and recedes into the background, becoming little more than an arbitrary pretext for the conflict. The subject's obsession pivots completely away from the object and locks entirely onto the mediator. The rival, initially just a model, becomes a looming, omnipotent figure in the mind of the subject, endowed with an almost divine aura. The struggle is no longer about acquiring a specific, tangible prize; it is about confronting the mediator's perceived god-like superiority.

This transition from mere physical wanting to a spiritualized fixation corresponds with striking fidelity to the classical Ayurvedic concept of _Bhūtonmāda_—exogenous psychiatric illness (_Āgantuja Unmāda_) in which the patient is seized by an invisible, non-physical entity (_Bhūta_ or _Graha_). Within our integrative clinical framework, the _Graha_ (literally, "seizer" or "captor") is diagnosed not as a medieval spook, but as the mental representation or _idea_ of the mediator's sovereign superiority, which violently seizes and colonizes the desiring subject's executive consciousness (_Buddhi_). We interpret the classical category of the _Bhūta_ as pathogenic archetypal memes and disembodied mimetic currents floating in the cultural atmosphere that the subject breathes. When certain predisposing conditions of vulnerability are met—above all, the existential exhaustion and ontological emptiness of an atomized ego—these trans-individual patterns take up residence within the host (_Graha-āveśa_), possessing their desires and dictating their behavior. The inherently _metaphysical_ nature of Girardian desire, which turns mortals into idols, thus finds its exact diagnostic equivalent in the metaphysical, nosological vocabulary of Ayurvedic _Bhūtavidyā_.

From the perspective of the desiring subject, the shift from mimetic to metaphysical desire is profoundly ontological. The subject feels acutely aware of their own internal emptiness, sensing a deep-seated lack of being or authenticity. Looking at the mediator, who appears fiercely autonomous and self-assured, the subject projects a mythical fullness onto this rival. The subject becomes convinced that the mediator possesses a hidden, transcendent quality—a metaphysical essence—that makes them complete and untouchable. The object of desire is now pursued not for its physical utility or beauty, but because it is seen as a tangible emanation of the mediator's divine essence. By acquiring the object, the subject secretly hopes to absorb the mediator's perceived fullness, thereby curing their own internal void. The geometry of imitation thus morphs into a desperate, magical quest to steal another human being's soul (_jīvātman_).

Ultimately, this pursuit of metaphysical essence traps the subject in a self-destructive cycle of perpetual enslavement. Because the perceived divine essence of the mediator is an illusion generated entirely by the subject's own mimetic fixation, it can never actually be acquired. Even if the subject successfully captures the disputed object, the anticipated ontological transfiguration fails to materialize; the object is immediately discarded as useless, and the subject's internal emptiness remains painfully intact. Driven by this agonizing failure, the subject seeks out ever more insurmountable rivals and increasingly punishing defeats, convinced that only a model who can consistently thwart them must truly possess the authentic essence they crave. The subject willingly subjugates themselves to the mediator, transforming their imitation into a grotesque form of religious worship where the rival is simultaneously idolized as a god and hated as an executioner.

To fully capture this dynamic, Girard vigorously opposes the _romanesque_ to the _romantique_. In his terminology, a _romantique_ work clings to the illusion of spontaneous desire, cloaking the imitative reality of human longing in myths of authentic passion, individual autonomy, and heroic isolation. Conversely, a _romanesque_ work shatters this facade, brutally disclosing the triangular, borrowed nature of desire and exposing the protagonist's enslavement to a mediator. By defining these terms not merely as literary eras but as fundamental existential postures toward reality, Girard radically stretches the definition of the _romantique_. In his framework, this umbrella expands far beyond nineteenth-century poetry to include modern existentialism and even the philosophy of Friedrich Nietzsche. Despite his fierce anti-idealism, Nietzsche still desperately clings to the illusion of the fiercely autonomous, self-generating individual, remaining completely blind to the underlying mechanics of mimetic rivalry that govern human behavior and create the illusion of the Nietzschean Übermensch itself.

=== I.2.2 Jean-Michel Oughourlian's clinical approach

The clinical operationalization of Girardian mimetic theory was pioneered by French neuropsychiatrist Jean-Michel Oughourlian, Girard’s primary collaborator on the psychological and psychiatric dialogues of _Things Hidden Since the Foundation of the World_.#footnote[René Girard, Jean-Michel Oughourlian, and Guy Lefort, _Things Hidden Since the Foundation of the World_, translated by Stephen Bann and Michael Metteer (Stanford: Stanford University Press, 1987).] In his subsequent clinical works, notably _The Puppet of Desire_ (1991) and _The Genesis of Desire_ (2010),#footnote[Jean-Michel Oughourlian, _The Genesis of Desire_, translated by Eugene Webb (East Lansing: Michigan State University Press, 2010).] Oughourlian systematically dismantled the individualistic dogma of standard Western psychoanalysis and biological reductionism. Replacing the solipsistic Freudian ego with an "interdividual" psychology, he demonstrated that human consciousness does not generate desire from within an isolated monad; rather, the psychological self (_le moi_) is an emergent byproduct continuously sculpted, destabilized, and dictated by the borrowed desires of the mediator.

This interdividual framework yields a revolutionary diagnostic re-interpretation of severe affective instability, particularly bipolar disorder and manic-depressive cycles (see the diagnosis of writer Virginia Woolf's "bipolar disorder" in _McThelema_ section 3.3). Rather than treating mood swings as unprovoked endogenous chemical surges occurring inside an isolated brain, Oughourlian reframes manic and depressive oscillations as the structural consequences of the patient's fluctuating relational distance from their chosen model of being. The manic high does not represent authentic joy or spontaneous biological vitality, but rather what Oughourlian terms the _élan vers le médiateur_ (the intoxicating rush toward the mediator). During this phase of psychological inflation, the subject hallucinates that they have successfully merged with the idolized rival and absorbed their transcendent autonomy. Oughourlian captures this illusory summit:

#block[
  #set text(size: 10pt, style: "italic")
  #pad(left: 1.5em)[
    "The manic phase corresponds to a triumphant appropriation of the model’s being. The subject experiences an exalting inflation of the ego, convinced that the void has finally been filled by the mediator’s essence."#footnote[Jean-Michel Oughourlian, _The Puppet of Desire: The Psychology of Hysteria, Possession, and Hypnosis_, translated by Eugene Webb (Stanford: Stanford University Press, 1991), p. 162.]
  ]
]

However, because the perceived divine fullness of the model is merely a mimetic mirage, this artificial state of psychological omnipotence is unsustainable. The moment the coveted object fails to bestow supernatural completeness, or the mediator demonstrates cold indifference, mockery, or outright defeat, the manic scaffolding abruptly collapses. The subject is instantly cast back into the terrifying reality of their own internal deficiency. As Oughourlian clinically diagnoses the catastrophic reversal into melancholic depression:

#block[
  #set text(size: 10pt, style: "italic")
  #pad(left: 1.5em)[
    "Depression is the agonizing death of a desire that has crashed against the obstacle of the model... the subject is cast into an abyss of nothingness, convinced of his own total unworthiness while the mediator looms larger and more unreachable than ever."#footnote[Oughourlian, _The Genesis of Desire_, p. 114; see also _The Puppet of Desire_, pp. 163–166.]
  ]
]
In this depressive phase, the patient experiences what clinical manuals classify as severe melancholia, psychomotor retardation, and suicidal despair, but which an interdividual analysis unmasks as the agonizing withdrawal symptoms of failed metaphysical appropriation.

By establishing that the manic-depressive polarity is fundamentally governed by the relational dialectic between subject and mediator, Oughourlian provides the precise clinical bridge to Ayurvedic _Bhūtavidyā_. The patient's consciousness is literally possessed by an exogenous archetype that dictates their neurochemical and emotional reality. When the host feels favored by the possessing entity, they soar into manic grandiosity; when they feel cast aside by the entity, and the _Bhūta_-induced expectations are revealed as _mithyā_ after colliding with reality, they plummet into suicidal exhaustion. Far from being a purely endogenous organic flaw, the bipolar oscillations observed across contemporary clinical psychiatry represent the somatic and affective footprint of a mind violently colonized by a competitive human deity.

=== I.2.3 History of Religion — The central Bhūta-pattern repository

Across six millennia of Eurasian religious history, the complex interplay of political-religious appropriation or rejection of sexual symbolism has created what is today the primary reservoir of cultural _Bhūtas_. As detailed across Chapters 4 to 8 of _McThelema_, this history revolves around a structural dialectic between two sacred poles of authority legitimation: the paternal, legalistic "_Kullaba_" and the martial-erotic "_E-Anna_". These two conceptual names are derived from the earliest archaeologically attested manifestation of the double-pole split in Late Chalcolithic Uruk (fourth millennium BCE), dividing the city between the towering sky-god Anu (the administrative _Kullaba_ precinct, ruled by the scribe-priests and the _ensi_) and the horizontal complex of Inanna-Ishtar (the martial-erotic goddess of the _E-Anna_ precinct, blessing the warlord _lugal_ and his predatory warband). Early empires alternately leaned into pure _E-Anna_ rule—as when Sargon of Akkad, a dynastic usurper, based his universal military conquests exclusively upon being "Ishtar’s lover" until his dynasty collapsed under Naram-Sin’s hubristic self-deification—or achieved a durable synthesis, perfected by Hammurabi of Babylon, who wrapped the kinetic Ishtarian fist (recharged annually through the _hieros gamos_ at the _Akitu_ festival) within the righteous, legalistic glove of Shamash’s monumental code.

In the Indo-Iranian and Mediterranean worlds, this institutional tension perpetually re-emerged as an existential battle between paternal assemblies and charismatic warbands. In Central Asia, the pastoralists’ sky-father Dyaus Pita was superseded by the severe, omniscient Varuna to contain the disruptive cattle-raiding warbands of Indra, culminating in Zarathustra’s puritanical revolution in the Hindu Kush, which banished martial deities as demonic _daivas_ to forge a pure Kullaba faith. Within the Indic sphere, the same ethos was carried by the _Bhargava_ tradition culminating in the equally uncompromising _Paraśurāma_. In Graeco-Roman antiquity, oligarchic councils of retired warlords (the Athenian _Areopagus_ and Roman _senatus_) sought to freeze society into Apollonian predictability, only to be repeatedly shattered by the return of the _E-Anna_: Peisistratos seized Athens by parading a living woman (Phye) dressed as Athena to simulate divine martial-erotic endorsement; the Ptolemaic dynasty fused Hellenistic tax bureaucracy with Egyptian sibling-incest and weaponized luxury (_tryphe_) under Isis-Aphrodite; and Julius Caesar mobilized Gallic legions by broadcasting direct descent from Venus Genetrix (the Roman Ishtar-analogue), establishing an autocracy that Augustus stabilized by veiling Venusian military power beneath the modest, Apollonian facade of the restored Republic.

In the Middle East, the rise of the Abrahamic faiths initiated a systemic, apocalyptic declaration of war against this martial-erotic sovereignty engine (akin to the _Bhargava_ tradition but with more complete success). Prophetic literature, culminating in the Book of Revelation, aggressively stripped away imperial majesty to unmask the imperial state as the "Wh\*\*\* of Babylon" (a near direct copy of Babylonian Ishtar), intoxicated with ecstatic _porneia_ (martial-erotic orgiastic frenzy), and projected an eschatological New Jerusalem without a temple—a pure _Kullaba_ utopia (closely paralleled by what _Paraśurāma_ Brahmins tried to establish in the Konkan) governed solely by the Paternal Lawgiver. However, because multi-ethnic empires still required kinetic military coercion, the _E-Anna_ could not be eradicated; it was simply driven underground into "the black" (unofficial but universally known worship). While Sassanid Persia maintained the last overt "red" synthesis of the Middle East—depicting the King of Kings receiving his royal glory (_khvarenah_) directly from the water-and-war goddess Anahita (syncretized with Mesopotamian Ishtar) alongside Ahura Mazda—the Islamic Caliphate forced the martial-erotic dynamic into the covert sphere by walling it off inside the private _harem_ (sharing its etymological root with _ḥaram_, that which is set apart as sacred or _sacer_), allowing the Caliph to operate as an unadulterated Mesopotamian warlord while projecting the abstract, legalistic majesty of Allah through the _ulama_ and _sharia_.

In Western Europe, following the collapse of centralized Roman administration, early medieval monks from Auxerre (France) reinvented the archaic Indo-European trifunctional schema (the three orders: _oratores_, _bellatores_, and _laboratores_, i.e., the three primitive Varnas) to pragmatically harness localized feudal violence through "coalitions of the meek" (the Peace of God). Yet, the repressed _E-Anna_ flared up repeatedly in violent episodes of "Sargonitis"—most vividly in the scandalous, boastful poetry of Duke William IX of Aquitaine, who painted his mistress on his battle shield, and later in Emperor Frederick II’s Neoplatonic, harem-flanked court at Palermo, which provoked papal encyclicals condemning him as the _preambulus Antichristi_.#footnote[A Latin theological designation meaning "forerunner of the Antichrist." In 1239, Pope Gregory IX deployed this phrase in his encyclical _Ascendit de mari bestia_ against Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II, recognizing the Emperor's self-sustaining, Neoplatonic court at Palermo as an existential challenge to ecclesiastical legitimacy.] To escape ecclesiastical annihilation, the feudal aristocracy invented a brilliant psychological technology: "stealth religion." Catalyzed by Eleanor of Aquitaine (Queen of France) and the troubadours,#footnote[The troubadours were high-medieval lyric poets from Occitania (southern France) whose vernacular songs formalized the ethos of courtly love (_fin'amor_), establishing an aristocratic cult of devotion toward an idealized, unattainable married Lady.] raw carnal conquest was sublimated into courtly love (_fin'amor_), elevating the woman into an idealized, chaste, and unattainable _midons_ (lord). By cordoning off this intense erotic idolatry under the protective alibi of "secular entertainment" and lyrical poetry, the nobility created a massive juridical loophole that the Church’s inquisitorial mechanisms were ill-equipped to prosecute.

This courtly "stealth religion" is precisely the historical cradle of what Girard diagnoses as the _romantique_ delusion. By transferring the religious devotion previously owed to the transcendent Father onto an idealized human creature, courtly love birthed the modern myth of spontaneous, autonomous, and transcendent passion, veiling the mimetic and predatory reality of desire beneath an aesthetic of spiritual elevation. Through this mechanism, the religious traditions of the _E-Anna_ established themselves as the supreme historical repository of cultural _Bhūtas_: they generated either the overt, coercive warlord (the "Big D\*\*\* Macho Boss" or BDMB) or the sublimated, intoxicating idol-lady of romantic yearning. When Louis XIV (King of France) concentrated this virtual _E-Anna_ at Versailles—institutionalizing the official royal mistress (_maîtresse-en-titre_) and compelling the nobility to worship the King’s physical body through court spectacle—he finalized the secular transmission line that today supplies modern mass media, literature, and collegiate subculture with their most virulent and seductive possessing archetypes.

=== I.2.4 Application to teenage female cliques and the Cornell 7 case

The immense gravitational pull that draws a continuous stream of female undergraduates into collegiate fraternity group s\*\* encounters finds its deepest and most detailed explanation in Sections 3.6 and 9.11 of _McThelema_. Conventional sociological and criminological analyses remain paralyzed by the apparent contradiction of the act: why would an educated young woman voluntarily cross the threshold of a fraternity house to engage in an event almost certain to trigger social ruin? Through the Girardian lens, her arrival at the chapter house is not an act of hedonistic recreation, but a desperate metaphysical maneuver. Starved of authentic being within a hyper-individualist culture that has dismantled all vertical transcendence,#footnote[See section I.2.5 below.] the subject seeks to cure her own acute ontological emptiness by absorbing the perceived divine fullness of the fraternity's high-status male mediator-BDMBs. In _Deceit, Desire, and the Novel_, Girard identifies this precise psychological mechanism in the figure of _la coquette_:

#block[
  #set text(size: 10pt, style: "italic")
  #pad(left: 1.5em)[
    "The coquette’s indifference is not feigned… She desires herself. But she is incapable of desiring herself without the help of her lover. She uses the other’s desire to desire herself… She offers her body to the other’s desire in order to capture his divinity."#footnote[René Girard, _Deceit, Desire, and the Novel_, p. 105.]
  ]
]

In the modern collegiate habitat, the fraternity house stands as a commodified, McDonald's-ified survival of Crowley’s Abbey of Thelema—a "McThelema" sanctuary. By inserting herself into the highly choreographed, multi-partner encounter, the undergraduate high-status female student (the campus "Stacy") attempts to turn her own physical body into the supreme locus of fascination for a collective of high-status male mediators ("Chads").#footnote[In contemporary American digital slang and internet folklore, "Chad" designates the stereotypical hyper-masculine, socially dominant alpha male, whereas "Stacy" denotes his high-status, conventionally attractive female equivalent who sits atop collegiate social hierarchies.] In her intoxicated, mimetic trance, she hopes that the charismatic sovereignty and untouchable ease possessed by these campus "gods" will be transferred to her through her physical surrender. By becoming the exclusive object of desire for an entire brotherhood (like the Vedic _Rodasī_), she seeks to manufacture an unassailable sense of self-worth, using the collective lust of the warband-like frat group to finally desire herself.

Yet, exactly as Oughourlian’s psychiatric model dictates, this desperate theurgic#footnote[Theurgy, rooted in ancient Neoplatonism, refers to esoteric ritual practices intended to invoke and manipulate divine or metaphysical forces. In early twentieth-century occultism, Aleister Crowley adapted this concept into "Sex Magick," framing transgressive sexual rites as an operational technology designed to alter cosmic reality and actualize the practitioner's sovereign Will.] gambit is structurally doomed to collapse into an agonizing manic-depressive cycle. During the initial hours of the encounter, the student experiences the soaring, manic intoxication of the _élan vers le médiateur_—a fleeting, euphoric inflation wherein she feels she has successfully merged with the mediators' power and overcome the void. But as the night terminates in biological exhaustion, alcohol-induced sickness, and the cold indifference of the participants, the metaphysical scaffolding violently disintegrates. The morning after brings the catastrophic depressive crash: the physical act has conferred zero ontological transfiguration; the mediators’ perceived divinity has vanished into crude locker-room chatter; and the subject is cast back into an even more terrifying abyss of internal nothingness, compounded by the horrifying realization of her complete physical and social exposure (photos and videos may already be circulating on social media).

This individual clinical tragedy cannot be understood in isolation from the broader intra-female peer ecosystem that precedes and surrounds it. Long before arriving on campus, young women are inducted into the relentless status panopticon of the adolescent clique, where the "Queen Bee" functions as an all-powerful, possessing cultural _Bhūta_. Within this female hierarchy, social capital is distributed with totalitarian cruelty. The vast majority of teenage girls, relegated to the status of "unf\*\*\*able" _Unterfrauen_, are held in a state of psychological subjugation, sexual starvation, and perpetual shame. They are trained to view their exclusion from elite aesthetic desirability as a fundamental ontological defect, living under the permanent, terrified surveillance of female peers who enforce compliance through the threat of absolute ostracism.

Crucially, the Queen Bee who sits at the summit of this schoolyard pyramid is herself trapped in the exact same mimetic torment. Because her authority is not grounded in authentic being but in the precarious, borrowed admiration of her sycophants, she lives in constant dread of displacement. Her apparent autonomy is a fragile mask. The moment she transitions into a more competitive arena—graduating to a prestigious university or encountering an elite peer who is wealthier, better connected, or aesthetically superior—her sovereign aura instantly shatters. The former Queen Bee is violently reduced to the status of a frantic "Wannabe," subjected to the very same relational humiliation, body dysmorphia, and depressive devastation that she previously weaponized against others. The entire social architecture is an engine of reciprocal enslavement where every actor, regardless of rank, is tormented by the same spectral _Graha_.

It is against this unbearable backdrop of lifelong female surveillance and existential starvation that the fatal attraction of the fraternity ritual reveals its ultimate logic. Having discovered that conformity to the female clique yields only exhausting anxiety, and crushed by the cold panopticon of "the black" (covert horizontal worship), the young woman seeks an escape from the female gaze at any cost. Indoctrinated into the modern gospel of autonomous self-deification yet hollowed out by its empty promises, she walks into the fraternity house to enact an orgiastic liturgy of reputational martyrdom. By offering her body as a sacrificial vessel to the male warband, she attempts a radical, boundary-shattering transgression, choosing the swift, catastrophic violence of public consumption over the lingering, suffocating death of female social mediocrity. The overall mental-health cost of all this is of course extremely severe.

=== I.2.5 Conclusion: The Necessary Restoration of Vertical Transcendence

The profound lesson of Girardian anthropology is that when "vertical transcendence"—the soul's direct, hierarchical orientation toward the Supreme Reality (_Paramātma-bhaktiḥ_)—is systematically erased from a culture, human longing for the absolute does not vanish with it. Denied an authentic vertical outlet, the religious impulse undergoes an inevitable horizontal displacement into "deviated transcendence" (_transcendance déviée_). In our integrative Sanskrit nosology, this pathology is designated as _Bhūta-bhaktiḥ_: the catastrophic misdirection of worship toward finite beings, social rivals, and cultural phantoms. As the _Bhagavad Gītā_ warns in its foundational diagnostic axiom:

#block[
  #set text(size: 10pt, style: "italic")
  #pad(left: 1.5em)[
    "yānti deva-vratā devān pitṝn yānti pitṛ-vratāḥ \
    bhūtāni yānti bhūtejyā yānti mad-yājino 'pi mām" \
    \
    "Those who worship the demigods go to the demigods; those who worship the ancestors go to the ancestors; those who worship the _bhūtas_ [finite creatures / spectral idols] go to the _bhūtas_; but those who worship Me come unto Me."#footnote[_Bhagavad Gītā_ 9.25.]
  ]
]
To worship a _mithya_ ghost (_bhūta_) is inevitably to be seized and colonized by a phantom (_bhūta_ i.e. _graha-āveśa_).

When this _Bhūta-bhaktiḥ_ operates covertly within the secular panopticon of "the black"—where open idolatry is officially prohibited by egalitarian pieties—it generates an environment of intense psychological toxicity. Because mortals can never embody the absolute fullness projected onto them, the devotee's worship is continually thwarted, collapsing into reciprocal envy, ressentiment, and depressive despair. As demonstrated throughout the Cornell 7 analysis and collegiate subculture, this unacknowledged horizontal religion is currently destroying the psychic stability of Western youth, who oscillate violently between the megalomaniacal highs of mimetic inflation and the suicidal nadirs of metaphysical bankruptcy.

Crucially, it would be a grave diagnostic error for Indian clinicians or cross-cultural psychiatrists to dismiss this phenomenon as mere "Western decadence" or an exotic foreign pathology. These Western-originated _Bhūtas_—pathogenic cultural memes of unbridled self-deification, hyper-individualism, and predatory relational consumption—have become autonomously self-replicating behavioral software. Uncoupled from their original geographic coordinates, they are transmitted seamlessly via global digital infrastructure, Hollywood (or Bollywood) streaming services, and social-media algorithms. They are already firmly implanted in upper-middle class school and university subcultures worldwide and possess the capacity to infect any host population that has abandoned its traditional transcendant anchors. The clinical task of contemporary psychiatry cannot remain confined to pharmaceutical symptom management; it urgently demands the restoration of authentic vertical transcendence to purge the mind of its destructive possessing _grahāḥ_.

== I.3. Western Culture specifics

=== I.3.1 Christianity's Internalized Kullaba

The apocalyptic literature of early Christianity (as mentioned above in section I.2.3), crystallized in the Book of Revelation, initiated an uncompromising polemic upon the ancient Near Eastern martial-erotic sovereignty engine, in amanner comparable to the _Bhargava_ assault on the Kshatriya ethos symbolized by _Paraśurāma_. By explicitly identifying the Roman imperial system with the "Wh\*\*\* of Babylon" (Ishtar), intoxicated with ecstatic _porneia_ and arrayed upon a scarlet beast (modelled on the Ishtarian lion, similar to Durga's, who is herself polemically attacked by _Paraśurāma_ as _Renuka_ or _Parvati_), the text tore away the grandeur of the state to expose its underlying predatory mechanics. In the vision of the New Jerusalem (Rev 21), there is conspicuously no temple, which is to say, no _E-Anna_ precinct to generate charismatic martial-erotic energy. Instead, society is projected as an eschatological utopia governed solely by the direct, unmediated presence of the Paternal Lawgiver and the Lamb. When the anticipated _parousia_#footnote[Second coming of Christ] was delayed, however, this revolutionary blueprint was not abandoned; rather, it was systematically internalized into the believer's introspective life.

This psychological relocation was given its classical formulation by Augustine of Hippo,#footnote[Augustine of Hippo (354–430 CE), Bishop of Hippo Regius, was the foundational theologian of Western Latin Christianity. His autobiographical _Confessions_ pioneered introspective self-examination, while _The City of God_ systematically contrasted the Earthly City (governed by the _libido dominandi_) with the City of God (governed by transcendent love and grace).] who transferred the _Kullaba_ from the public forum into the individual human psyche. In _The City of God_, Augustine unmasked the Roman obsession with martial-erotic glory as the _libido dominandi_ (the lust for domination, the direct predecessor of Nietzsche's "will to power"), demonstrating that an earthly society built upon self-love inevitably collapses into mimetic destruction. Through the _Confessions_, in which he exposes his own temptations, he formulated an unprecedented technology of introspective self-surveillance, training the individual to relentlessly interrogate their internal desires and subordinate the unruly impulses of "the flesh" (i.e. the Ishtarian impulse) to the gaze of the Divine Father. The "coalition of the meek" was thereby converted into the default psychological posture of the Christian soul, bound together not by external coercion, but by _agape_—the divinely repurposed social-mammal cohesion instinct#footnote[This evolutionary psychology interpretation is of course not explicit in ancient Christian texts, but a close examination of the texts reveals that this is what inspired them.]. Interestingly, Augustine describes the idolatrous impulse in terms that closely parallel Girardian notions and the language of Gita 9.25. He calls it _aversio a Deo_ (turning away from God) followed by _conversio a creatura_, i.e. turning towards a _Bhūta_.

This introspective warfare against internal Ishtarian _libido dominandi_, which we term "_porneiomachia_#footnote[Literally, "fighting the whore", an ancient Greek term coined to parallel other similar terms in early Christian theology like _pneumatomachia_, _eikonomachia_, _logomachia_, etc.]", recognized that so long as the erotic component remained active within the soul, the charismatic gravity of the warlord archetype could always resurrect itself. By methodically starving the martial-erotic drive of its mimetic fuel, the internal _Kullaba_ neutralized transgressive impulses before they could manifest as civic tyranny or physical violence. Before this introspective technology could be generalized across the wider populace, it required centuries of concentrated institutional incubation within the medieval monastery. Under the _Rule of St. Benedict_, the rigorous division of the liturgical day, continuous surveillance of thoughts, and the systematic practice of humility turned the cloister into a controlled laboratory for the suppression of the _libido dominandi_.

During the Protestant Reformation, John Calvin dismantled the cloister walls, exporting this elite monastic software directly to the entire lay population and turning the whole of society into an open-air monastery. Calvinism maximized the thermodynamic efficiency of the internal _Kullaba_ by asserting the total depravity of the human will, demanding an unrelenting, hyper-vigilant state of self-examination against the slightest stirring of the _E-Anna_ drive. In the _Institutes_ (his chief work), Calvin characterizes this permanent internal tribunal:

#block[
  #set text(size: 10pt, style: "italic")
  #pad(left: 1.5em)[
    "It is like a watchman that God has set over man, to observe and spy out all his secrets, so that nothing may remain buried in darkness."#footnote[John Calvin, _Institution de la religion chrestienne_ (1560), IV.10.3. This is echoed in Madhvacharya's enjoining of constant vigilance against _kartṛtva-ahaṃkāra_ for example in the _Gītā Bhāṣya_ and _Gītā Tātparya Nirṇaya_ on Gītā 3.27.]
  ]
]
To enforce this inward vigilance, Calvin urged an unceasing spiritual conflict against the natural inclinations of the soul, declaring in his sermons:

#block[
  #set text(size: 10pt, style: "italic")
  #pad(left: 1.5em)[
    "We must wage a continual and mortal war against our flesh, and against its lusts, which are like wild beasts seeking to devour us."#footnote[John Calvin, _Sermons sur l’Épître aux Éphésiens_.]
  ]
]

Because this Calvinist architecture of internal policing was extraordinarily robust, the external charismatic monarch became structurally obsolete. A commonwealth of self-policing, radically internalized _Kullaba_ subjects had no functional need for an _E-Anna_ warlord to maintain social order; on the contrary, it viewed charismatic, transgressive monarchs as intolerable idols. This dynamic drove the systematic deposition, execution, or constitutional neutering of sovereigns across Reformed Europe—from Geneva to Cromwell’s England and the Puritan commonwealth of colonial Massachusetts. Yet, this total victory was conducted in a state of ideological sleepwalking: by reducing the Wh\*\*\* of Babylon strictly to an anti-papal polemic (thus forgetting the more general annti-Ishtar intent of the original polemic), Protestant theologians lost sight of the ancient Near Eastern physics of the martial-erotic engine, leaving the modern secular state vulnerable to the very mimetic forces it ceased to recognize.

=== I.3.2 Max Weber's reading of Protestant modernity

The profound economic and institutional fallout of this psychological transformation was famously diagnosed by Max Weber in _The Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism_.#footnote[Max Weber, _The Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism_, translated by Talcott Parsons (London: Allen & Unwin, 1930), pp. 98–128.] Weber demonstrated that the internalization of monastic discipline produced "inner-worldly asceticism"—the systematic redirection of human energy away from the ecstatic, zero-sum plunder of martial-erotic glory and into rational, methodical, and continuous labor. This represents the exact thermodynamic mechanism of the ancient _Kullaba_ operating at civilizational scale, functioning entirely through the internalized self-discipline of the population.

By neutralizing the _E-Anna_ drive for territorial conquest and replacing it with the predictable, mathematically accountable accumulation of capital, the internalized Protestant _Kullaba_ generated the modern liberal state and industrial capitalism. Modernity is precisely the vast, bureaucratic, and rationalized architecture that emerges when the charismatic warlord is permanently locked out of the system. Weber designated this rationalized order as the "iron cage"—the ultimate, unbreakable glove of legalistic predictability. In this respect, the Industrial Revolution functioned as the material realization of the Gospel pericopes of the multiplication of the loaves, generating unprecedented agricultural and economic abundance through the peaceful logistics of commerce, industrial production, and hygienic scale.#footnote[The miracle of the multiplication of the loaves and fishes is uniquely recorded across all four canonical Gospels. The clearest and most foundational account appears in Matthew 14:19–21: "Taking the five loaves and the two fish and looking up to heaven, he gave thanks and broke the loaves. Then he gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the people. They all ate and were satisfied, and the disciples picked up twelve basketfuls of broken pieces that were left over. The number of those who ate was about five thousand men, besides women and children."]

=== I.3.3 Post Modern Rebellion — Foucault

It is precisely this Weberian "iron cage" of internalized discipline that became the central target of the late-twentieth-century postmodern revolt of which thinkers like Michel Foucault#footnote[Michel Foucault, _Discipline and Punish: The Birth of the Prison_, translated by Alan Sheridan (New York: Pantheon Books, 1977), pp. 195–228.] became the archetypal embodyment. Foucault dedicated his intellectual career to deconstructing what he termed "pastoral power" and "panopticism," correctly identifying that the modern state controls its subjects not through the spectacular violence of the ancient monarch, but through the pervasive, internalized surveillance born of the Christian  _porneiomachia_. Crucially Foucault's critique was not neutral scholarship but a militant ideological insurgency aimed at dismantling this introspective policing mechanism specifically to "liberate" transgressive erotic desire from its moral constraints. Viewed through our framework, Foucault stands as the ultimate reactionary: his philosophy provided an elaborate intellectual rationale to tear off the _Kullaba_’s protective glove in order to facilitate a return to the unfettered worship of the martial-erotic warlord. Whether this was voluntary or an unintended consequence is ambiguous, as we shall see.

As Girardian mimetic theory anticipates, when a subject embedded in the context of Western Culture destroys the Christian framework, i.e. the only available form of _Paramātma-bhaktiḥ_ available to him, and abandons the internalized _Kullaba_, they do not achieve Rousseauian autonomy. Instead, they fall immediately into the mimetic abyss. Starved of vertical transcendence, the subject experiences an unbearable ontological void and undergoes a masochistic inversion, actively seeking subjugation and demanding a master brutal enough to prove his own sovereign autonomy (see section I.4.1 below). 

Foucault’s well-documented immersion in the sadomasochistic bathhouses of 1970s San Francisco#footnote[James Miller, _The Passion of Michel Foucault_ (New York: Simon & Schuster, 1993), pp. 245–285.] serves as the empirical verification of this dynamic: having intellectually dismantled the Paternal Lawgiver, his so-called "philosophical" quest for "limit-experiences" culminated in the literal physical desire to experience absolute domination, proving that the destruction of internal self-policing leads directly to the worship of the violent mediator (the _Asura-graha_ in masochistic form).

=== I.3.4 The Nietzschean Hegemony

The intellectual collapse witnessed in late twentieth century Western thought is deeply intertwined with the unacknowledged hegemony that the philosophy of Friedrich Nietzsche has exercised over Western social sciences, humanities, and clinical psychology since the Second World War. From Parisian existentialism and post-structuralism to contemporary critical theories, the Western academic clerisy has operated under the unexamined premises of the Nietzschean ethos: the assassination of God to emancipate the self, the systematic demolition of transcendent moral order as mere "slave morality," and the _romantique_ elevation of the autonomous will to power. In Section 9.10 of _McThelema_, this covert cultural religion is diagnosed in its two complementary, gendered expressions: "Male-Centric Nietzschean Self-Worship" (MCNSW) and "Female-Centric Nietzschean Self-Worship" (FCNSW).

The initial cultural reception of Nietzsche’s _Übermensch_ crystallized rapidly in the interwar and mid-twentieth century as MCNSW. This male-centric modality elevated the autonomous masculine will, unapologetic physical aggression, and the predatory solidarity of the martial warband. Historically, MCNSW animated the totalitarian horrors of the mid-twentieth century and survived in the postwar era as an underground cult of transgressive immunity within elite pipelines. In contemporary digital subculture, MCNSW re-emerges in the so-called "Manosphere," where young men explicitly idolize hyper-dominant alpha-male archetypes, celebrating unrestrained coercive swagger and the complete subjugation of societal and ethical boundaries.

Conversely, FCNSW emerged as the female psychological counterpart within late modern secularism. While Nietzsche himself was notoriously misogynistic, this posed no obstacle to the adoption of his core theological project: the pursuit of self-deification. In reality, FCNSW operates as disguised male-worship, driven by intense Girardian mimetic desire for the fantasized, MCNSW-constructed _Übermensch_. In the contemporary world of "the black," where overt submission to a dominant master is socially and politically prohibited by egalitarian taboos, this thwarted metaphysical longing cannot express itself openly. Consequently, as Girard diagnoses in _Deceit, Desire, and the Novel_, thwarted adoration inevitably curdles into intense, venomous hatred. This hatred-laced metaphysical desire manifests directly as the moralistic, punitive hostility that contemporary intersectional and academic discourse projects onto traditional authority.

This Nietzschean paradigm has thoroughly conquered Western clinical psychotherapy and counseling practices. Rather than assisting the patient in submitting their disordered ego to an objective, transcendent order (i.e. reality), modern therapeutic orthodoxy operates as a chaplaincy to expressive individualism. Under the influence of Abraham Maslow and Carl Rogers' humanistic psychology, therapy prioritizes "unconditional self-acceptance," self-actualization, and emotional validation, actively shielding the patient’s narcissistic ego from moral accountability. By treating the individual’s subjective feelings as the sole criterion of truth, modern psychotherapy merely reinforces the patient's delusion of autonomous sovereignty, leaving them defenseless against the subterranean forces of mimetic envy and relational rivalry. In other words, modern Western-style "therapy" is a massive engine of iatrogenic bipolar-like disorders.

Concurrently, the postwar educational apparatus has institutionalized this Nietzschean framework as the foundational dogma of modern pedagogy. Over the past fifty years, Western schools have systematically abandoned classical character formation and moral discipline in favor of therapeutic slogans: "empowerment," "self-esteem," "unleashing one's authentic self," and "positive reinforcement." Under the guise of fostering healthy child development, the educational system trains every student to view themselves as a self-originating sovereign center of value#footnote[In that domain, the worst offenders are the SEL-aligned (Social-Emotional Learning) initiatives like CASEL (Collaborative for Academic, Social, and Emotional Learning), the Fetzer Institute and so on.]. In the diagnostic categories of Ayurvedic _Bhūtavidyā_, modern schooling has officially set every child on the path of _Deva-graha Bhūtonmāda_—the delusional, megalomaniacal god-seizure wherein the individual acts pompous, demands uncritical reverence from their environment, and claims an autonomous, inviolable perfection.

Because finite human beings are inherently fragile, contingent, and mimetically dependent, this state of pedagogical self-apotheosis is thermodynamically unviable. When the student steps out of the sheltered classroom into the friction of real-world competition and peer hierarchies, the fragile _Deva-graha_ inevitably shatters. Starved of genuine ontological weight, the personality rapidly degenerates into secondary pathologies: the aggressive, ruthless rivalries of _Asura-graha_; the hyper-sexualized, cosmetic panopticon of _Gandharva-graha_; and ultimately the self-loathing, masochistic degradation of _Piśāca-graha_. The modern school system has thus functioned as a mass incubator for the very psychiatric epidemic now overwhelming contemporary adolescent mental health.

=== I.3.5 Conclusion: The Collapse of the Christian Sacred Canopy

Throughout its historical trajectory, Latin Western Christianity provided a remarkably potent, institutionalized formulation of vertical transcendence (_Paramātma-bhaktiḥ_). By establishing the internalized _Kullaba_ through Augustinian-Calvinist self-surveillance and monastic discipline, the Christian synthesis succeeded in systematically purging every form of overt pagan idolatry, martial-erotic divinization, and sacred transgression from the official public sphere. For over a millennium, this civilizational architecture functioned with exceptional thermodynamic efficacy: so long as the Christian sacred canopy remained structurally intact, human mimetic desire was tethered vertically to a transcendent Paternal Lawgiver, containing the volatile energies of the warband and stabilizing the social order beneath the rule of law.

However, this totalizing success harbored a fatal systemic vulnerability: Western civilization had, so to speak, placed all its transcendent eggs into a single theological basket. Unlike ancient India, which maintained parallel exoteric and esoteric avenues to the sacred (the Brahminical and Tantric syntheses), Western Europe tethered its entire psychological and ontological stability exclusively to the Christian revelation. Consequently, when the Christian framework collapsed under the combined weight of the Enlightenment, industrial secularization, and the postmodern revolt, the consequences were civilizationally catastrophic. The West did not merely revise its religious dogmas; it lost every legitimate cultural pathway to the transcendent. Yet, as Girard’s anthropology inexorably demonstrates, the human hunger for the absolute cannot be abolished by secular decree. Denied its beneficial vertical orientation, the metaphysical drive underwent a violent horizontal devolution, fracturing into two distinct modalities of deviated transcendence.

On the one hand, the secularized West witnessed a partial resurgence of idolatry "in the red," wherein the masses exploit the medieval "secular entertainment loophole" to openly worship screen celebrities, athletic icons, and billionaire demigods as surrogate deities. While culturally ubiquitous and highly visible, this commercialized fandom remains relatively benign compared to the far darker pathology operating "in the black." In this covert, horizontal sphere—where egalitarian taboos strictly prohibit overt master-worship—starved metaphysical longing curdles into unacknowledged, sado-masochistic peer rivalries, toxic mimetic envies, and destructive masochistic inversions (see I.4.1 below). As demonstrated by the Cornell 7 case and the ruthless panopticon of adolescent female cliques, it is this subterranean idolatry in "the black" that inflicts the most devastating psychological violence, colonizing the minds of modern youth with predatory cultural _Bhūtas_ and driving the unprecedented contemporary epidemic of psychic fragmentation.

== I.4. Terminal Girardian Decay

=== I.4.1 The Three Stages of Girardian Decay and the Turn to Demonic Masters

The erosion of vertical transcendence in the post-Christian West did not liberate the individual into autonomous, enlightened freedom; rather, as René Girard demonstrates in _Deceit, Desire and the Novel_, it triggered an inexorable, three-stage societal pathology of metaphysical desire. In the initial stage, as the Western elite began abandoning orthodox Christian faith, it sought to stave off the resulting ontological void without overtly regressing to the brute coercion of ancient martial-erotic despots. Modernity erected a pantheon of "positive," secularized idols operating within the stable sphere of external mediation ("the red"): the Sublime Artist, the solitary creative genius whose ego supposedly generates masterpieces _ex nihilo_; the Revolutionary Redeemer, born of the French Revolution, who promised that guillotining the traditional monarch would inaugurate an egalitarian utopia of civic virtue; the Scientific Genius, the intellectual priest whose mathematical mastery of nature promised absolute fullness of being; and the Screen Celebrity, revered by the public from a safe distance as a polished, modern demigod.

By the late twentieth century, however, the second stage—the "Great Disappointment"—inevitably set in, as every single one of these secular mediators suffered complete ontological bankruptcy. Within Girard’s geometric framework of desire, this collapse was unavoidable: because the perceived divinity of any human model is merely a mimetic projection generated by the subject’s own lack, any ideal that can be inspected or imitated at close range immediately loses its transcendent aura. The dynamic slides inexorably from distant admiration into the bitter, envious rivalries of internal mediation ("the black"). Across high culture, as artists recognized that aesthetic beauty could not deliver metaphysical transfiguration, they turned against art itself, embracing ironic, subversive anti-art and deliberately vandalizing aesthetic standards. Concurrently, the secular revolutionary utopia collapsed into bureaucratic surveillance, totalitarian terror, and the Gulag; the cult of the Scientific Genius stalled as fundamental theoretical physics hardened into untestable academic hyper-specialization; and omnipresent digital media dissolved the protective aura shielding the celebrity, exposing the cultural idol as fragile, manufactured, and hollow.

This universal disillusionment propelled Western culture directly into the third and terminal stage of mimetic decay: the masochistic inversion. When every benevolent, rational, and lawful model has been exposed as an impotent fraud, the modern subject’s pride (_vanité_) stubbornly refuses to abandon metaphysical longing and submit to the transcendent God. Instead, the subject executes a perverse ontological deduction: if lawfulness and moral goodness are merely the marks of weak, domesticated clerks (as Friedrich Nietzsche proclaimed), then the only way a mediator can prove that he genuinely possesses sovereign, untouchable autonomy is by being openly cruel, transgressive, and above the law. As Girard diagnoses:

#block[
  #set text(size: 10pt, style: "italic")
  #pad(left: 1.5em)[
    "The subject turns toward the only master who can still fascinate him, the one who makes him suffer... The masochist asks his executioner to prove to him that he is a god."#footnote[René Girard, _Deceit, Desire, and the Novel: Self and Other in Literary Structure_, translated by Yvonne Freccero (Baltimore: Johns Hopkins Press, 1965), p. 178.]
  ]
]
Once society crosses this threshold, its unacknowledged cultural religion sheds its polite humanist pretenses, mutating into the explicit, sado-masochistic adoration of the transgressive master—a landscape where only a cruel executioner (_bourreau délicieux_) can simulate a spark of the sacred.

In the diagnostic nosology of classical Ayurvedic psychiatry, this terminal masochistic turn represents the acute manifestation of _Bhakti-vibhrama_—the radical perversion and misdirection of the soul's innate devotional capacity, cataloged in the _Caraka Saṃhitā_ (_Cikitsāsthāna_ 9.20) as a defining feature of _Unmāda_. Starved of its true object in the Supreme Person (_Paramātman_), the patient’s _Bhakti_ is projected onto spectral human mediators (_Bhūtas_). The subject craves the mediator's contaminated leftovers (_kūṭa-prasāda_), which the _Bhagavad Gītā_ (3.13) describes as "eating one's own sin." In this state of acute psychic colonization, the individual invites physical degradation, emotional torment, and social humiliation from their idol, using suffering as a somatic anesthetic to confirm that an absolute power still exists, thereby sealing their complete cognitive fragmentation under an armada of competing _Grahas_.

=== I.4.2 The Literary Arc: From the Byronic Hero to Dark Romance

This two-hundred-year descent into terminal mimetic sickness is transparently recorded in the historical evolution of Western romantic literature, which bridges the gap between medieval courtly love and contemporary mass-market fiction. While high-medieval _fin'amor_ placed the male knight in a posture of chaste, reverent submission before an idealized, married Lady (_midons_), nineteenth-century Romanticism violently inverted the gender polarity of this "stealth religion." As the romantic exaltation of the autonomous human hero collided with the perceived boredom of bourgeois Christian morality, the idealized male mediator rapidly darkened into a cruel, Luciferian figure. In _The Romantic Agony_, Italian literary critic Mario Praz traced the philological lineage of this "Metamorphosis of Satan": John Milton’s defiant Lucifer in _Paradise Lost_ was transmuted through eighteenth-century Gothic villains directly into the literary persona of Lord Byron:

#block[
  #set text(size: 10pt, style: "italic")
  #pad(left: 1.5em)[
    "From Milton's Satan to the Gothic villain, and finally to the Byronic hero, the rebel against God and society acquires a sinister, irresistible fascination: it is precisely his crime, his cruelty, and his damnation that endow him with a superhuman, fatal majesty in the eyes of his victims."#footnote[Mario Praz, _The Romantic Agony_, translated by Angus Davidson (London: Oxford University Press, 1933), p. 61.]
  ]
]
When Lady Caroline Lamb famously branded Byron (her lover) in 1812 as "mad, bad, and dangerous to know," she articulated the modern formula of transgressive worship: he was desired not in spite of his cruelty and disdain for common morality, but precisely because of them.

This dark, predatory archetype was immediately domesticated and installed at the heart of female romantic fiction by Charlotte and Emily Brontë. Long before composing _Jane Eyre_ and _Wuthering Heights_, the sisters spent their adolescence constructing secret imaginary paracosms (_Angria_ and _Gondal_) centered on despotic, Byronic warlords. When Emily Brontë published _Wuthering Heights_ (1847), she stripped the Byronic Satan of aristocratic polish and presented Heathcliff as raw, kinetic violence—an "incarnate goblin" and "fiend" whose freezing, sadistic cruelty toward Isabella Linton only intensifies her masochistic compulsion to submit to him:

#block[
  #set text(size: 10pt, style: "italic")
  #pad(left: 1.5em)[
    "Tell her what Heathcliff is: an unreclaimed creature, without refinement, without cultivation; an arid wilderness of furze and whinstone... He’s not a rough diamond—a pearl-containing oyster of a rustic: he’s a fierce, pitiless, wolfish man."#footnote[Emily Brontë, _Wuthering Heights_ (London: Thomas Cautley Newby, 1847), ch. 10, p. 226.]
  ]
]

While the nineteenth-century English novel still draped this predator-worship in poetic moorland mist, the twentieth-century French literary avant-garde systematically tore away the romantic veil to expose the unalloyed sadomasochistic mechanics beneath. Drawing directly upon the Marquis de Sade—"rehabilitated" by post-WWII French intellectuals like Simone de Beauvoir, Georges Bataille, and Michel Foucault as a philosopher of radical human liberation—Dominique Aury (writing as Pauline Réage) published _Histoire d’O_ (_Story of O_) in 1954.#footnote[Pauline Réage, _Story of O_, translated by Sabine d’Estrée (New York: Grove Press, 1965).] In this text, a Parisian woman is delivered by her lover to an aristocratic estate where she is whipped, branded, and passed around an elite fraternity of masters. Crucially, Réage frames O's physical and psychological degradation not as abuse, but as an ascetic, quasi-monastic state of grace—an inverted theurgy wherein the annihilation of the female ego beneath the whip of the collective brotherhood provides the profound ontological peace that modern secular autonomy could never deliver.

By the early twenty-first century, this French esoteric prototype had been thoroughly democratized, commercialized, and exported to global audiences. The transition was marked by high-brow memoirs such as Toni Bentley's _The Surrender: An Erotic Memoir_ (2004),#footnote[Toni Bentley, _The Surrender: An Erotic Memoir_ (New York: ReganBooks, 2004).] in which absolute physical submission to an emotionally detached lover (christened "the A-Man") is evangelized as a literal religious epiphany, and culminated in the 165-million-copy explosion of the _Fifty Shades of Grey_ franchise. Today, contemporary commercial fiction is dominated by the multi-billion-dollar "Dark Romance" genre—novels that churn out endless variations of billionaire sadists, mafia executioners, and demonic captors who stalk and dominate the heroine into psychological surrender. Already in 1872, in _Demons_, Fyodor Dostoevsky had prophetically constructed Nikolai Stavrogin as a devastating _romanesque_ deconstruction of this exact idol, demonstrating that the worshipped "badass" is merely an empty monster whose icy detachment leaves a wake of suicides and ruined lives. Yet, despite Dostoevsky’s prophetic warning and Girard’s clinical autopsy in the early 60s, Western culture has enthusiastically embraced the predator as its primary romantic ideal.

=== I.4.3 The Guru-Hero Arc and Elite Predatory Cults

In strict parallel with this literary mutation, the countercultural quest for spiritual enlightenment outside traditional religion followed the exact same three-stage Girardian trajectory. In the 1950s and 1960s, as the Beat Generation gave way to the counterculture, Stage 1 manifested as a naive fascination with Eastern mysticism, communal harmony, and the benevolent guru. Writers like Jack Kerouac popularized the idea that esoteric spiritual rites could gently dissolve the bourgeois ego. However, because these self-appointed teachers operated completely outside the ethical and regulatory safeguards of classical Asian traditions or Western moral frameworks, the utopian ideal collapsed rapidly into Stage 2 cynicism and Stage 3 masochism. By 1969, this dynamic produced its first catastrophic prototype in Charles Manson, who weaponized psychoactive substances and mandatory group sexual rites to forge a murderous, fanatical retinue of followers entirely subservient to his charismatic commands.

By the dawn of the twenty-first century, this countercultural guru apparatus had discarded its communal trappings, migrated into the upper echelons of corporate, political, and technocratic influence, and bifurcated along class lines into two structurally homologous, predatory cults: Jeffrey Epstein’s network at the apex of the global ruling class, and Keith Raniere’s NXIVM organization among affluent professionals and media figures. When analyzed comparatively, the institutional blueprint uniting these two operations is virtually identical:

